 A planning regression proves all parts occur exactly once on one-, four-
 and twelve-core machine plans, including an explicit file requested
 alongside --all. This changes scheduling only, ADR 0262 case A.
+
+## Amendment: admit fitting work behind a blocked job (2026-11-14, #1762)
+
+The weighted pool chooses the longest queued job that currently fits both
+its CPU and memory budgets. Waiting only for the queue head can strand
+capacity: a one-core job holds half a two-core pool while a two-core job
+blocks another one-core job behind it. The memory budget has the same
+head-of-line problem.
+
+The existing sorted queue and resource accounting remain the single owners.
+No validation jobs are removed, no timeout is extended, and a job larger
+than the budget still runs alone. Two real-process regressions require the
+smaller job to release the running job before the larger one may start;
+they failed before backfilling and pass after it. This proves admission,
+not a controlled speed-up for the entire compiler suite.
+
+Resource accounting is derived from a set of active reservations, not
+independently incremented/decremented totals. Fractional core estimates can
+leave a positive floating-point residue after every child has exited;
+using that residue to decide whether an oversized job may run alone
+deadlocks an empty pool. A third bounded subprocess regression reproduces
+this with 0.1- and 0.2-core jobs before an oversized reservation. An empty
+reservation set now has exactly zero usage, with no tolerance or timeout
+escape hatch.
